    ],
    "resultsInterpreter": [
      {
        "display": "Michael Tanaka, MD"
      }
    ],
    "conclusion": "1. No acute traumatic injury in the abdomen or pelvis.\n2. Lower anterior abdominal wall contusion.\n3. 3.4 cm right adnexal cyst with a thin internal septation. Recommend nonemergent pelvic ultrasound for further characterization.",
    "presentedForm": [
      {
        "contentType": "text/plain",
        "text": "EXAM:\nCT ABDOMEN AND PELVIS WITH INTRAVENOUS CONTRAST\n\nCLINICAL INDICATION:\nMotor vehicle collision, restrained driver, airbag deployment. Lower abdominal pain and seatbelt mark. Evaluate for traumatic injury.\n\nTECHNIQUE:\nHelical CT of the abdomen and pelvis in the portal venous phase after intravenous administration of 100 mL iohexol 350. Coronal and sagittal reformats were reviewed. CTDIvol: 11.2 mGy. DLP: 548 mGy-cm.\n\nCOMPARISON:\nNone.\n\nFINDINGS:\nLower chest: Lung bases are clear. No pneumothorax.\nSolid organs: No laceration or hematoma of the liver, spleen, pancreas, kidneys, or adrenal glands.\nBowel and mesentery: No bowel wall thickening, mesenteric hematoma, or free air.\nPeritoneum: No free fluid.\nPelvic organs: The uterus is normal. 3.4 cm cystic lesion of the right ovary with a single thin internal septation; no solid component or mural nodularity. The left ovary is normal with physiologic follicles. The bladder is unremarkable.\nVessels: No vascular injury.\nBones: No fracture.\nSoft tissues: Mild subcutaneous stranding of the lower anterior abdominal wall, consistent with seatbelt contusion.\n\nIMPRESSION:\n1. No acute traumatic injury in the abdomen or pelvis.\n2. Lower anterior abdominal wall contusion.\n3. 3.4 cm right adnexal cyst with a thin internal septation. Recommend nonemergent pelvic ultrasound for further characterization."
      }
    ]
  },
